// endpoint_report_page_unloading.proto — ReportPageUnloading: Emacs is about
// to unload this workspace's page ON PURPOSE. HOST section.
//
// UNLOADING A PAGE IS A PERFORMANCE MEASURE, NEVER A STATE THE USER SEES
// (owner ruling, 2026-10-20). Emacs unloads the page of a workspace nobody has
// looked at for a while (page-unload.el), and the page's WatchWebWorkspace
// stream ends with it. Without this report the daemon read that end as a
// broken web hop (daemon.md invariant 11) and drew a working workspace blue
// until the user switched back to it.
//
// THE REPORT PRECEDES THE UNLOAD. Emacs unloads the page only once this call
// has answered success, so the daemon knows the end is deliberate before the
// stream can end: no ordering between the two is left to chance. A call that
// fails leaves the page loaded.
//
// WHAT IT MEANS TO THE DAEMON: the workspace's web hop is AWAITED, not broken,
// until a WatchWebWorkspace stream for it opens again (the switch back that
// mounts a new page). An awaited hop is never drawn as a fault. The same
// awaiting stands from the moment a workspace's host stream opens until its
// page first connects, so neither a restart nor a handover draws a page that
// has not yet reached the new daemon as broken; only a page that WAS
// connected and went away without this report is.
//
// IDEMPOTENT: reporting an unload again changes nothing.

